                    A rule built only from prohibitions becomes fragile because it leaves desire staring at the forbidden object. Christian temperance begins with a larger yes: presence to God, faithful relationships, health, meaningful work, Sabbath, beauty, generous use of resources, and joy that can be shared. Each restraint should name the good it protects. Turning off a device protects attention at dinner; limiting alcohol protects judgment and health; a spending pause protects stewardship; measured speech protects truth and communion.

                    The cardinal virtues work together. Prudence reads circumstances and selects fitting means. Justice remembers other people's claims. Fortitude sustains the good through discomfort and failure. Temperance orders appetite so pleasure does not seize the whole field of choice. Faith, hope, and charity carry these acquired dispositions into life with God. A rule of enough should therefore never be only a private optimization system. Its fruits appear in worship, availability, responsibility, and love.

                    Review the domains studied: bodily pleasure, food and drink, media and novelty, possessions and status, speech and anger, work and rest, fasting and feasting. Choose only the few areas where a clear rule can presently serve a real good. Trying to control every appetite at once often produces a dramatic beginning and concealed collapse. A limited practice sustained truthfully is more formative than a severe plan designed for an idealized self.
