                    Completion of a course is evidence of study, not certification of virtue. Do not convert checked lessons, streaks, fasting days, purchases avoided, or screen minutes into a spiritual rank. Quantities can provide useful facts, but the deeper fruits are freedom, peace, truthfulness, responsibility, gratitude, worship, and love. A person may keep a numerical rule while becoming proud or harsh; another may adapt a rule for health while growing in genuine self-mastery.

                    Failure becomes material for return. Name it precisely, receive mercy, make needed repair, identify the occasion, and resume at the next concrete opportunity. Do not demand a dramatic restart that postpones ordinary fidelity. Celebrate real growth without claiming independence from grace. At four weeks, keep, revise, or retire each practice according to its fruits and current vocation. Temperance remains lifelong because good things continue to invite love, and love continually teaches the measure of their use.
